  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed conversion would result in the loss of a 3-bedroom family dwelling and fails to provide adequate compensatory family accommodation, leading to a net reduction in family housing within the borough. This outcome undermines the Council's strategic objective to increase the supply of larger (3+ bedroom) units, which are essential to meeting the housing needs of families within the borough. The proposal is therefore contrary to Section 5 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2026), Policy GG4 of the London Plan (2021), Policy CP5 of the Core Strategy (2010), Policies DMD4 and DMD5 of the Development Management Document (2014), and the Council's Strategic Housing Market Assessment (SHMA 2015).",
    "The proposed development due to its poor provision of natural light and outlook to the ground floor bedroom; in addition to a kitchen in the second floor bedroom would result in a substandard form of accommodation detrimental to the living conditions and residential amenity of future occupants of the self-contained flats. The development is therefore contrary to the National Planning Policy Framework (2026), Policies D3 and D6 of the London Plan (2021), Policies CP4 and CP30 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010) and Policies DMD8 and DMD37 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The proposed development, in the absence of an adequate flood risk assessment and sustainable drainage strategy, fails to demonstrate how proposed measures would ensure that the development is safe from flooding, will not increase flood risk elsewhere and utilises Sustainable Drainage at the site. The proposal is therefore contrary to the NPPF (2026), Policies SI 12 and SI 13 of the London Plan (2021), CP21 and CP28 of Core Strategy (2010) and DMD 59, DMD 60, DMD 61 of the Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The proposed development, due to insufficient provision of private amenity space, particularly the first-floor flat which would not be provided with any private outdoor amenity space, would result in substandard and inappropriate accommodation incapable of meeting the reasonable needs of future occupiers. The proposal is therefore contrary to the National Planning Policy Framework (2026), Policy DMD8(g) and DMD9 of the Development Management Document, and the minimum private amenity space standards of Policy D6 in London Plan."
  ],
